# Copy to .env and fill in. This file is never committed.

# --- Database (PostgreSQL, local) ---
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=change-me
POSTGRES_PORT=5432

# --- Web interface ---
# The one password that protects the interface, even for a single user.
DINDON_PASSWORD=change-me-too
DINDON_PORT=8000

# --- Local AI (Ollama) ---
# On a Mac, run Ollama natively (it can use the GPU, Docker cannot): see README.md.
# On a Linux server, use the "ollama" profile of docker-compose.yml and set http://ollama:11434.
OLLAMA_URL=http://host.docker.internal:11434

# --- Discord: collection (leave empty to only read the exports that you drop in inbox/) ---
# A bot token is recommended. Automating a personal account is against Discord's terms of service
# and can get it closed: see the warning in README.md.
# DISCORD_TOKEN=
# One or more server IDs, separated by commas
# DINDON_GUILD_IDS=
# Seconds between two looks at what is new (30 to 60 is polite; the delay before something shows is about half of it)
# DINDON_POLL_SECONDS=30
# Threads exported with their channel when an account is used: none, active or all
# DINDON_THREADS=active

# --- Discord: the live bot (mode C) ---
# Uses the same DISCORD_TOKEN and DINDON_GUILD_IDS, which must then be the token of a BOT and the server(s) to follow.
# In the Developer Portal: Bot > Privileged Gateway Intents > switch on "Message Content Intent"; invite the bot to the server
# with the permissions "View Channels" and "Read Message History". Start it with: docker compose --profile bot up -d --build
# To try the bot alone, switch the watcher off (otherwise both bring the same messages, which is harmless but hides the bot):
# DINDON_COLLECTOR=off
